--TEST--
Core\Jwt::sign and Core\Jwt::verify agree over a sweep of claim shapes — an empty bag, JSON text held as one claim, non-ASCII in a name and a value, the integer bounds and a 2048-character claim all come back as the claim that went in, counted — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key` and `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, counted. `sign` assembles the payload as text by hand so the
// claims keep their insertion order (crates/nvs-stdlib/src/jwt.rs's
// `payload_of` owns why), and `verify` reads it back through a real JSON
// parser. Two different pieces of code, one question asked of both: is the
// claim on the way out the claim that went in? A member that re-encoded,
// coerced or truncated anything harder than a flat bag of short ASCII still
// round-trips `["sub" => "ada"]` plausibly and fails the counts below.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();

// 2048 characters, which is past any fixed buffer a hand-assembled serializer
// would have reached for.
string $long = Core\Str::repeat("ada-", 512);

array<array<string>> $shapes = [
    // No claims at all — the one shape where the payload is the registered
    // pair and nothing else, and so the one that catches a separator written
    // before it is needed.
    [],
    // The flat bag every other case already asks about, here so the sweep
    // contains the shape that passes trivially.
    ["sub" => "ada"],
    // A claim whose value is itself JSON. It has to come back as one claim
    // holding that text, not as an object the parser flattened into claims of
    // its own: the module doc's *a claim is text* section is why there is no
    // second reading of it.
    ["profile" => "{\"roles\":[\"admin\",\"ops\"],\"tenant\":{\"id\":7}}"],
    // Non-ASCII in both halves. A name and a value go through the same
    // escaper, so a name is as much a test of it as a value is.
    ["nom" => "Ada Lovelace — Grüße, 東京, 🔐", "hôte" => "acme"],
    // The integer bounds, one past them, and a padded number: text a
    // serializer tempted to coerce would hand back in a different spelling
    // while every one of these still looks like a number.
    ["min" => "-9223372036854775808", "max" => "9223372036854775807", "past" => "9223372036854775808", "padded" => "007"],
    // Length.
    ["blob" => $long]
];

int $signed = 0;
int $counted = 0;
int $claimsIn = 0;
int $identical = 0;
foreach ($shapes as array<string> $bag) {
    string $token = Core\Jwt::sign($bag, 15m, $key);
    array<tainted string> $back = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
    $signed = $signed + 1;
    $claimsIn = $claimsIn + (Core\Arr::count($bag) as int);
    // The caller's claims and exactly the two the member owns — a shape that
    // gained or lost one fails here rather than on the value comparison.
    if ((Core\Arr::count($back) as int) == (Core\Arr::count($bag) as int) + 2) {
        $counted = $counted + 1;
    }
    foreach ($bag as string $name => string $value) {
        if ($back[$name] == $value) {
            $identical = $identical + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $signed, " shapes signed, ", $counted, " of them verify back to the caller's claims plus the registered pair", "\n";
echo $identical, " of ", $claimsIn, " claims are the claim that went in", "\n";

// The long one reported as a length rather than as a boolean, so a truncation
// says what it truncated to. Nothing here echoes a claim: they come back
// `tainted` (`rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`), and `Core\Str::length` is the neutral member that
// answers about one without carrying a byte of it.
array<tainted string> $blob = Core\Jwt::verify(Core\Jwt::sign(["blob" => $long], 15m, $key), $key);
echo "the ", Core\Str::length($long), "-character claim comes back ", Core\Str::length($blob["blob"]), " long", "\n";
--EXPECT--
6 shapes signed, 6 of them verify back to the caller's claims plus the registered pair
9 of 9 claims are the claim that went in
the 2048-character claim comes back 2048 long
